<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><td id="gutterContainerId-35"><div data-block-id="35" id="b35"><p>Greetings, Gentle Member.</p><p><em>The Onion</em> prides itself on being a self-sufficient entity, even going so far as to making our own pens that use the EZ-flowing blood of interns as ink. Now, however, we are asking for your assistance.</p><p>Certain issues of <em>The Onion</em> newspaper are mysteriously missing from our 270-year archive, and you might be able to help us recover them.</p><p>Here are the issues we're looking for:</p><ul><li><p>All of Vol. 14 <strong>(1988) </strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 15 <strong>(1989)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 16 <strong>(1989)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 17 <strong>(1990)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 18 <strong>(1990)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 19<strong> (1991)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 21 Issue 12 <strong>(1992)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 22 Issues 4, 5, 7, &amp; 16 <strong>(1992)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 29 Issues 19 &amp; 22 <strong>(1996)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 33 Issue 9 <strong>(1998)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 39 Issue 5 <strong>(2003)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 42 Issue 5 <strong>(2006)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 44 Issues 49-52 <strong>(2008)</strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 45 Issues 1-20 &amp; 33-43 <strong>(2009) </strong></p></li><li><p>Vol. 46 Issues 3-52<strong> (2010)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 47 <strong>(2011)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 48 <strong>(2012)</strong></p></li><li><p>All of Vol. 49<strong> (2013)</strong></p></li></ul><p>If you have any of these issues and are willing to part with them, we’d potentially be interested in purchasing them from you so that they can be properly archived.</p><p><a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdVhIdw8vKhSc1Hc27kAY6mwTFbi8QCyn-_kqJZUpxpSJ1LAg/viewform?usp=header" target="_blank">Complete this form if you have issues in your possession that fill these gaps. </a></p><